  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indicator 1: Lead time (week or months) of early warnings with warnings validity
Indicator 2: Number of automated agro-meteo stations/posts for network, and small agro-meteo for districts installed
Indicator 3: Status of phenological, pest and plant disease forecast adapted to local conditions (YES/NO)
Indicator 4: Number end-users (% of women-led) in Fergana Valley received long-term climate-change information and used it for strategic planning of agricultural fruit and vegetable production</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2022">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">Baseline 1: Lead time of 6 months and validity 70%
Baseline 2: 0
Baseline 3: No
Baseline 4: Farmers, rural households, and agro-producers lost 15-30% of fruit and vegetable production due to abnormal weather events and lack of early warnings and long-term climate- information (2015)</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Target 1: Lead time of 8 months and validity 75%
Target 2: 15 automated agrometeorological stations and post installed is part of network; and 10 small agrometeorological stations installed in target districts 
Target 3: Yes
Target 4: At least 300 end-users (10% of women-led) in Fergana Valley received long-term climate-change information and used it for strategic planning of agricultural fruit and vegetable production</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Target 1: 15 automated agrometeorological stations (AMS) procured to expand the current network of hydrometeo and agrometeo station in Fergana Valley. Expended network will be capable to produce early warnings with at least 6-month lead time and validity of 70% or higher for the local target beneficiaries (horticultural producers, farmers, and agro business and cooperatives). A concept was formulated on establishing the Agrometeorological Situational Centres in Fergana Valley regions equipped with videoconferencing facilities for online communication with Uzhydromet departments to get access to real time agrometeo data and early warnings to undertake preventive response actions. 

Target 2: 15 automated weather stations and associated IT equipment (15 sets Desktop Personal Computers, Monitors and peripheral devices) for AWS procured and delivered to Tashkent. 20 small agro-meteo stations procured and installed (5 in Andijan region and 8 in Fergana region and 8 in Namangan region)..

Target 3: YES. 118 (24% of participants are female) national specialists represented the Agency of Plant Protection and Quarantine, Uzhydromet, Meteoinfocom, Gidrometeorological research institute, farming from Fergana Valley familiarized with the climate change problems faced by Uzbekistan and learned about drafting garden protection schemes against pests and diseases, vegetable crops, varieties and hybrids, development of protection and nutrition programs for vegetable crops, winter agricultural activities and preventive measures in gardening, and were provided with recommendations on how to disseminate information through horticultural channels within 5 capacity building events (workshop and trainings) conducted in Tashkent with participation of professors from Kyoto, Kobe, Ibaraki Universities (Japan) from SATREPS project, and UNDP projects staff and in Fergana Valley regions during September-November 2022.

Target 4: An introductory video, which visualises how climate change affects yields and product quality, as well as the importance of having access to agrometeorological data in order to avoid the negative effects of climate change was developed for horticultural producers and demonstrated November 2022. Also, infographics explained how the climate change impacts affect horticultural production developed and disseminated (Video was posted on UNDPs Facebook, Telegram, Youtube, Twitter, Instagram and also on Pr.uz (media) with total reach of 410 views, Infographics was posted on Facebook and Telegram with total reach of 620 views)</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
